# Credentials for the acceptance matrix (npm run acceptance / acceptance:seed).
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed and
# must never hold a real value.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Nothing else in pr-review reads this. The product's providers read only
# process.env — the acceptance runner loads this file and injects the values
# into the CLI it spawns, so a test-only convenience stays out of the shipped
# tool. Precedence: a real environment variable > this file >
# ~/.pr-review/acceptance.env > the provider CLI logins (gh / az / glab).
#
# In CI none of this applies: the workflow maps its `acceptance` environment
# secrets onto the same variable names, and no file exists.

# GitHub — only needed if you are not logged in with `gh auth login`.
# For CI this is a fine-grained PAT scoped to the fixture repo:
#   Pull requests: Read and write · Contents: Read-only
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# Azure DevOps — PAT from https://dev.azure.com/{ORG}/_usersSettings/tokens
#   Code: Read & write · Pull Request Threads: Read & write
AZURE_DEVOPS_PAT=

# GitLab — personal access token, scope `api`.
# `api` is the only scope that can POST /merge_requests/:iid/discussions;
# there is no narrower write scope. A project access token needs Premium.
GITLAB_TOKEN=

# GitHub Copilot CLI — a token belonging to an identity WITH a Copilot seat.
# Kept separate from GITHUB_TOKEN on purpose: that one is scoped to the
# fixture repo and has no Copilot access, and the Copilot CLI would fall back
# to it (its precedence is COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN -> GH_TOKEN -> GITHUB_TOKEN).
# Locally, `gh auth token` is usually the right value. In CI this is not set
# at all — the workflow passes ${{ github.token }}, which carries
# copilot-requests from the permissions block.
COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN=
